    "turn_text": "The values that I was raised with and the culture I was raised with absolutely shapes my agenda. It shapes the way that I approach policymaking and relationship building, and it influences my priorities. You know, so as you've mentioned, you know, I grew up in El Paso, I'm a third-generation El Pasoan. I grew up in a really big extended family. I have four brothers, but my mom had a dozen siblings. And so I grew up with lots of theas and theos, with lots of primas and primos and uncles and cousins. And we were a family and we are a family that's very united, a family that takes care of each other. a family that really everything we do is deeply rooted in love. And when I was growing up, I found that that was true throughout my community, that that was who El Paso is. And so the being shaped with that perspective about leading with love, about making sure that no one gets left behind, about taking care of one another, That's the perspective that I bring to Congress. And I think it is absolutely an El Paso perspective. I think it's a Latina perspective and it is my North Star.",
