  {
    "episode_id": 44461,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44461.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "I never imagined I would serve in Congress. It was never something I dreamed of doing. I never even really imagined I would be in public service. I taught English at the local community college in El Paso, and at the University of Texas at El Paso, UTEP, my alma mater. And I loved my career and my profession, and I loved engaging with my students And I thought I was set that that was the career path I would follow, but I was really motivated. I read somewhere that while men are mostly motivated by wanting to serve in the office itself, most women who run for office are motivated by an issue. And I definitely have been motivated by the issues. It's what made me get out of my chair and knock on doors for other candidates. It's what made me want to fundraise for other candidates. And so it was just this natural progression and some pushing from friends and allies that got me to run for office in the first place when I ran for County Commissioner way back when. And it was a decision that I'm so grateful I made and I'm so grateful to everyone who supported me and still supports me because it's",
