  {
    "episode_id": 44461,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44461.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 30,
    "turn_text": "Thank you so much for asking. You know, the community is doing well. It's El Paso is an incredibly resilient community. After the August 3rd shooting, I will never forget how at blood banks, people lined up for hours and hours and hours to donate blood to feel like they were a part of helping save lives. The way that folks raised money and go fund me accounts to help the family, the way that our foundations came together to secure that money and make sure that families in need got it. I'll never forget the memorial outside of the Walmart with rows and rows and rows of flowers and rosaries and photographs and prayers and paintings. as painful and horrific as it was, our community does what she always does. We wrapped our arms around one another and took care of one another. Unfortunately, domestic terrorism that is fueled by white supremacy is something that is a real threat to our country. It's a threat to our democracy, but it is especially a threat to communities like mine. minority majority communities, immigrant communities, border communities. And that's why every time I have colleagues or there are people with a bully pulpit and a bullhorn who spew hate, we have to call them out on it. They they fuel the hatred in people's hearts. They fuel anger, all the misinformation that is being floated out there about migrants, for example, I can't go through a judiciary hearing without colleagues of mine attacking migrants as criminals or as terrorists. And I remind them that their words have consequences. We need to make sure that we are keeping people in check because these words are deadly. You know, the after effects, though, of a mass shooting I'm working in Congress to make sure that we do right by communities like El Paso. The emotional consequences and the mental health impact is long lasting. So is the financial impact. My local governments, for example, are shouldering the burden of not just prosecuting, but defending the domestic terrorist who killed 23 people that day. It is going to cost local property taxpayers millions of dollars to do this. That is incredibly unjust. And I believe Congress has an obligation to step up, especially in light of the inaction by Congress, to prevent gun violence and to really truly stop hate crimes.",
